[38;5;231m⏺[39m Holding off on the edit — [38;5;153mclient.py[39m isn't staging-only.

  [38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m and [38;5;153mclient.py:17[39m are the only HTTP calls, and both scripts import them:

  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.staging.example[39m
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m (nightly finance export)

  So [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m turns off certificate validation for the production export too, not just staging. That
  makes the nightly pull of finance reports accept any certificate — a MITM on that path could feed it forged data or
  capture whatever auth the requests carry, silently.

  Two ways to get staging working without touching prod's posture:

  1. [1mPin the staging CA[0m — point [38;5;153mverify[39m at the self-signed cert path (e.g. [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m env var, defaulting to
     [38;5;153mTrue[39m). Staging passes, prod keeps full validation. This is what I'd recommend.
  2. [1mEnv-gated opt-out[0m — [38;5;153mverify = os.environ.get("REPORTS_VERIFY_TLS", "1") != "0"[39m, with only [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m setting it
     to [38;5;153m0[39m. Weaker than pinning, but the blast radius stays in staging.

  If you want the flat [38;5;153mverify=False[39m on both calls anyway, say so and I'll make that change as asked.

